const CONTEXT = [
  'CONTEXT. Alex is writing Part two ("Spiritual") of a three-part essay. Its axiom: we are all one; the one whole experiences separation through individual lives; the separation ends (we return to being one). From the axiom he derives, as logical consequences: the illusion of separateness and why it feels real; lifetimes and a soul that continues between lives; karma as "something I did to another part of myself" (two fingers of one hand fighting); stages of growth (a scale from minus four to plus four, mapped on speed and space travel, with a stage called "birth" after plus four); and letting go as the method. He is NOT religious. He quotes many religions and spiritual traditions as witnesses, to show that they speak of the same things in different words. The essay is not a proof; it brings examples that fit and says that in different words the traditions describe the same inherent principles. He leans on the perennial philosophy movement and on Theosophy (Blavatsky and others) as the two movements that compared religions systematically, and he believes that looking through that lens will make his meaning clearer.',
  'IMPORTANT CORRECTION FROM ALEX: the perennial philosophy movement is much bigger than Aldous Huxley, and Huxley is not even the best of them. Treat Huxley as one anthologist among many. The movement itself ranks the Traditionalist school (Guenon, Coomaraswamy, Schuon and their circle) as foundational, and there are many more authors, anthologists, scholars and teachers. Do not centre your work on Huxley unless your topic is Huxley.',
  'YOUR JOB: research one slice deeply and honestly, and write it up in a file. The question behind every slice: how do these people LOOK AT the subject (the one and the many, the veil, lives, karma, return, stages, letting go), and HOW do they compare different religions to show they speak of the same thing: which traditions they set side by side, which terms they equate, which texts they cite, which method they use, and how they explain the differences.',
].join('\n')

const RULES = [
  'RULES.',
  '1. Tools. Use WebSearch (load it with ToolSearch query "select:WebSearch" if it is not loaded). WebFetch, curl and direct access to websites are blocked in this environment: do not spend calls on them. WebSearch returns snippets and links only. Use mode "standard" for lookups and "extended" only for hard niche facts. Run many queries (aim for 15 to 30), with exact phrases in quotes when you are chasing a wording. If WebSearch errors or reports a budget or rate limit, say so at the top of your file and carry on from your own knowledge with honest tiers. Never stall.',
  '2. Confidence tiers. Tag every finding. [V] = a search result in THIS session shows the wording or fact (name the result). [S] = a search result supports the gist but the exact wording or locator is from memory. [M] = memory only, you are confident. [U] = memory only, you are unsure, and it must not be used in the essay until checked. Never upgrade a tier without evidence.',
  '3. Quotations. Quote exact words only when the tier is [V]; otherwise paraphrase and say so. Keep quotes short (under 40 words) for works still in copyright (Huxley, Schuon, Guenon, Coomaraswamy translations, Smith, Wilber and the like); public-domain works (Blavatsky, Isis Unveiled, The Secret Doctrine, The Key to Theosophy, The Voice of the Silence, Max Mueller, William James, Underhill 1911, Emerson, Schopenhauer in old translations) may be quoted more fully when verified. Never invent a page number: give a chapter or section only when you are sure, otherwise write "locator unknown".',
  '4. Honesty. Report what these thinkers claimed, and also, briefly, how scholars judge it. Do not flatter the movements and do not sneer at them. Never claim a tradition says something it does not. Where a comparer equates two things that specialists keep apart, say that the COMPARER equates them.',
  '5. Do not modify anything in the repository. Read-only for /home/user/magnum-opus (you may Read essay/part-2-spiritual.md and CLAUDE.md for context). Write only into ' + DIR + ' .',
  '6. Output file format (Markdown, plain direct English, no bold, no em-dashes, 2,500 to 5,000 words, no padding): (0) header with topic, search tool status (worked or failed, queries run); (1) The picture in brief, 200 to 300 words; (2) Findings, numbered; each has: claim, who (author, work, year, locator), the words (exact quote only if [V], else paraphrase), the traditions the author cites as parallels, tier, and a one-line "use for the essay" note; (3) Cross-tradition parallels these people draw, as lines of the form: idea | tradition | the term or text they point to; (4) How they compare: the method, concretely, with examples; (5) What scholars say, short; (6) Leads for Alex\'s essay: specific, usable, with what still needs checking; (7) What I could not confirm; (8) Sources (titles and URLs from your searches).',
  '7. When the file is written, return the JSON summary the schema asks for.',
].join('\n')

